  "limitations": [
    {
      "title": "Cross-sectional design",
      "text": "The analysis uses one year (2022) of country-level data, so it shows how countries differ at a point in time; it cannot establish temporal ordering or causal effects."
    },
    {
      "title": "Country-level (ecological) inference",
      "text": "Relationships between country averages do not necessarily hold for individuals within countries, and small territories count equally with populous countries."
    },
    {
      "title": "Missing data",
      "text": "8 countries were removed because GDP per capita or life expectancy was missing (Cuba; Eritrea; Gibraltar; St. Martin (French part); Korea, Dem. People's Rep.; South Sudan; British Virgin Islands; Yemen, Rep.). Their income groups are: Low income 4, Upper middle income 1, High income 3, so the missing cases are not a random sample and the cleaned data may under-represent some settings (50% of the dropped countries are low income vs 10% of the analysed countries). 21 health-expenditure and 12 CO2 values are also missing and were excluded pairwise."
    },
    {
      "title": "Confounding",
      "text": "GDP per capita and life expectancy are both linked to healthcare access, education, public infrastructure, demographics, environmental conditions and institutional quality. These are possible explanations for the association, not mechanisms tested here."
    },
    {
      "title": "Outliers and influential observations",
      "text": "Extreme observations - notably Central African Republic at 18.8 years - can shift correlations and regression estimates. The sensitivity analysis shows how much: Across the 4 scenarios Pearson r (log GDP) ranges from 0.766 to 0.859 (largest change from the baseline 0.792: 0.067) and the slope from 9.2 to 10.3 years per ten-fold GDP. The direction is unchanged but the strength label varies (strong positive / very strong positive), so the size of the association depends partly on unusual countries."
    },
    {
      "title": "Source revisions",
      "text": "World Bank WDI values are revised as new estimates arrive. Re-running the extraction later may give slightly different numbers than the 2026-09-18 extraction reported here; the code, source, year and extraction date are recorded so the method is reproducible."
    }
  ],
  "automated_summary": ["Data: 295 economies were extracted from the World Bank API on 2026-09-18; after removing 78 aggregates and 8 countries missing core values, 209 countries and 13 variables remain.", "Missingness: 33 of 2717 cells (1.21%) are missing in the cleaned data, all in health_exp_per_capita_usd (21) and co2_per_capita_t (12); core variables are complete.", "Life expectancy (2022): mean 73.1 years, median 74.2, ranging from 18.8 (Central African Republic) to 85.7 (Monaco), SD 7.9.", "GDP per capita: mean US$21,201 but median only US$7,656 - a strongly right-skewed distribution (min US$302 in Burundi, max US$226,052 in Monaco).", "Groups: mean life expectancy is highest in North America (80.2 yrs) and lowest in Sub-Saharan Africa (63.1 yrs); high-income countries average 79.1 yrs vs 61.1 yrs for low-income countries (gap 18.0 yrs). ANOVA across regions: F(6, 202) = 34.0, p = 3.4e-28, eta-squared = 0.50.", "Correlation: log GDP per capita and life expectancy show a strong positive correlation (Pearson r = 0.79, 95% CI 0.73 to 0.84, p = 3.6e-46; Spearman rho = 0.85). This is an association, not proof of causation.", "Regression: each ten-fold increase in GDP per capita is associated with 9.9 more years of life expectancy (95% CI 8.8 to 10.9, p = 3.6e-46); R^2 = 0.63, adjusted R^2 = 0.62.", "Outliers (1.5 x IQR rule): 16 high GDP-per-capita outlier(s) above US$66,552, 1 low life-expectancy outlier(s) below 51.5 years (Central African Republic) and 6 high fertility outlier(s); all were retained.", "Sensitivity: Across the 4 scenarios Pearson r (log GDP) ranges from 0.766 to 0.859 (largest change from the baseline 0.792: 0.067) and the slope from 9.2 to 10.3 years per ten-fold GDP. The direction is unchanged but the strength label varies (strong positive / very strong positive), so the size of the association depends partly on unusual countries.", "Outputs: 4 required figures and 1 additional figure generated; automated data QA PASS (28 of 28 completed check